  "short_a": "6.4.120 removes the reduplicant and gives e instead of short medial a between single consonants before the relevant weak perfect endings. 6.4.121 extends the same to seṭ thal. N/ṇ and s/ṣ substitutions independent of liṭ do not violate the restriction on initial substitutions caused by liṭ itself. The Kāśikā gives nematuḥ and sehe. Initial dentalization in the two naṭ entries and class-one ṣam therefore does not block e.",
  "māh": "Long ā lies outside the short-a prescription. Strong P 3sg mamāha is also the 3sg of short mah, but 3du mamāhatuḥ and mehatuḥ are distinct.",
  "different_second_singulars": "Thal of nam can omit iṭ under the competing prescriptions 7.2.62–63 or take iṭ with e and loss of the copy under 6.4.121. The ā-final styai entries under 7.2.61–63 preserve ā in the unaugmented choice and lose it before iṭ under 6.4.64. The k/ṅit weak vowel-initial positions also lose ā, while ṇal gives au under 7.1.34. The consonantal reduplicant is t, 7.4.61.",
  "derived": "A primary ām-form has an independent auxiliary. Kṛ follows the primary entitlement by 1.3.63; as/bhū appear in P in the received construction. At the final nasal before c/b both anusvāra and homorganic consonant are selected as in PG208. Before initial ā of as the retained spelling is m.",
